        "math_academy_id": "question-009",
        "problem": "Let $n$ be an integer. Prove that $a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$ if and only if $a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ and $a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:* \n\n$$\n2\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1=\\text{gcd}(2,5)=1\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "The connector \"if and only if\" means we must prove an equivalence. \n\n$$\nP\u21d4Q\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of proving an equivalence directly, we can instead prove two conditional statements. \n\n$$\nP\u21d4Q\u2261(P\u21d2Q)\u2227(Q\u21d2P)\n$$\n\nSo, we start our proof as follows:\n\nWe need to show  the following:\n\n$$\na\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\\,\u21d4\\,(a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\u2227\\,a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5))\n$$\n\nThis is the same as proving two implications.\n\nFirst, we consider the \"left to right\" implication.\n\n** Case $\u21d2:$** We need to show that \n\n$$\na\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\\,\u21d2\\,(a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\u2227\\,a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)).\n$$\n\nTo do that, we first write the statement in terms of divisibility. \n\nOr, in terms of divisibility,\n\n$$\n10\u2223a-b\\,\u21d2\\,(2\u2223a-b\\,\u2227\\,5\u2223a-b).\n$$\n\nThen, we write $n=a-b$ to simplify the notation. \n\nLet's denote $n=a-b.$\n\nSo, we now need to prove that \n\n$$\n10\u2223n\\,\u21d2\\,(2\u2223n\\,\u2227\\,5\u2223n).\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\nIf $10\u2223n,$ then $n=10k$ for some integer $k.$ We can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\nn=2(5k),\n$$\n\nwhich means that $2\u2223n.$ Also, we can write\n\n$$\nn=5(2k),\n$$\n\nwhich means that $5\u2223n.$ Therefore, $2\u2223n$ and $5\u2223n.$\n\nNext, we consider the \"right to left\" implication.\n\n**Case $\u21d0:$** We need to show that\n\n$$\n(a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\u2227\\,a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5))\\,\u21d2\\,a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).\n$$\n\nOr, in terms of divisibility,\n\n$$\n(2\u2223a-b\\,\u2227\\,5\u2223a-b)\\,\u21d2\\,10\u2223a-b.\n$$\n\nAgain, we simplify the notation by introducing $n=a-b.$ \n\nLet's again denote $n=a-b.$\n\nSo now, we need to prove\n\n$$\n(2\u2223n\\,\u2227\\,5\u2223n)\\,\u21d2\\,10\u2223n.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the definition of divisibility.\n\nSince $2\u2223n,$ then $n=2s$ for some integer $s,$ and since $5\u2223n,$ then $n=5t$ for some integer $t.$\n\nNotice that the divisibility of $n$ by $2$ and $5$ are independent. So, in general, we should use different letters ($s$ and $t$) in the reasoning above. \n\nNext, we'll use the fact that our divisors are coprime.\n\nThe integers $2$ and $5$ are coprime and $2\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1=1.$ So, multiplying this equation by $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n2\\cdot (-2n)+5\\cdot n=n.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $n=5t$ into the first term on the left-hand side and $n=2s$ into the second term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (-2(5t))+5\\cdot (2s)=n \\\\ 10\\cdot (-2t)+10\\cdot (s)=n \\\\ 10\\cdot (-2t+s)=n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $10\u2223n.$ So, the second implication is proved.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\nTherefore, $a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$ if and only if $a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ and $a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).$\n\nPROOF\n\nWe need to prove the following:\n\n$$\na\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\\,\u21d4\\,(a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\u2227\\,a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5))\n$$\n\nThis is the same as proving two implications.\n\n** Case $\u21d2:$** We need to show that \n\n$$\na\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\\,\u21d2\\,(a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\u2227\\,a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)).\n$$\n\nOr, in terms of divisibility,\n\n$$\n10\u2223a-b\\,\u21d2\\,(2\u2223a-b\\,\u2227\\,5\u2223a-b).\n$$\n\nLet's denote $n=$ $a-b$.\n\nIf $10\u2223n,$ then $n=10k$ for some integer $k$. We can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\nn=2(5k),\n$$\n\nwhich means that $2\u2223n$.  Also, we can write\n\n$$\nn=5(2k),\n$$\n\nwhich means that $5\u2223n$. Therefore, $2\u2223n$ and $5\u2223n$.\n\n**Case $\u21d0:$** We need to show that\n\n$$\n(a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\,\u2227\\,a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5))\\,\u21d2\\,a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).\n$$\n\nOr, in terms of divisibility,\n\n$$\n(2\u2223a-b\\,\u2227\\,5\u2223a-b)\\,\u21d2\\,10\u2223a-b.\n$$\n\nLet's denote $n=$ $a-b$.\n\nSince $2\u2223n,$ then $n=2s$ for some integer $s$, and since $5\u2223n,$ then $n=5t$ for some integer $t$.\n\nThe integers $2$ and $5$ are coprime and $2\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1=1.$ So, multiplying this equation by $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n2\\cdot (-2n)+5\\cdot n=n.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our expressions for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\cdot (-2t+s)=n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $10\u2223n$. So, the second implication is proved.\n\nTherefore, $a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$ if and only if $a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ and $a\u2261b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).$",
